From 00c922a443a6d99c6773f2bc4fd17d7a37be9d5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Remora <noreply@localhost>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 07:14:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A17 container-compat: sandbox-proof memfd detection in
 libcutils ashmem
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Sandboxed WebView renderers rendered black (Play sign-in, Geekbench results,
any WebView): every shared-memory region they received failed validation and
the renderer could not draw (bd remora-4ei.76).

Two compounding defects in ashmem-dev.cpp:

1. is_memfd_fd() identified a memfd only via readlink(/proc/self/fd/N), which
   Chromium's renderer sandbox denies with EPERM — and with sys.use_memfd=true
   a memfd is exactly what every region is. Ask the fd itself first:
   F_GET_SEALS answers >= 0 for every shmem-backed fd (memfd with or without
   MFD_ALLOW_SEALING) and EINVAL for ashmem's chardev fd and ordinary files.
   The /proc probe remains as fallback. All four is_memfd_fd() callers
   (ashmem_valid, get_size, set_prot_region, pin/unpin) are repaired at once.

2. The ashmem fallback could never work anyway: get_ashmem_device_path()
   probed /dev/ashmem<boot_id>, a node this container never creates — the
   existing container-compat patch opens plain /dev/ashmem in __ashmem_open() but left
   the rdev probe on the suffixed path, so __init_ashmem_rdev() failed
   image-wide ('Unable to stat ashmem device'). Probe the plain path, which
   also drops a /proc read a sandboxed process cannot perform.

Verified live on the running A17 instance: the failing pair
  readlink(/proc/self/fd/N) failed: Operation not permitted
  Unable to stat ashmem device: No such file or directory
storms from com.android.webview:sandboxed_process0 on every WebView load, and
stat of the suffixed path fails even unsandboxed. F_GET_SEALS discrimination
verified against memfd (sealed + unsealed), a chardev and a regular file.

Change-Id: I8557863d2e4d12aefe739ad0bf77a5ae9a311e6d
---
 libcutils/ashmem-dev.cpp | 26 +++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libcutils/ashmem-dev.cpp b/libcutils/ashmem-dev.cpp
index 7fdec30df..bca3a5965 100644
--- a/libcutils/ashmem-dev.cpp
+++ b/libcutils/ashmem-dev.cpp
@@ -111,15 +111,12 @@ bool use_memfd() {
 }
 
 static std::string get_ashmem_device_path() {
-    static const std::string boot_id_path = "/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id";
-    std::string boot_id;
-    if (!android::base::ReadFileToString(boot_id_path, &boot_id)) {
-        ALOGE("Failed to read %s: %m", boot_id_path.c_str());
-        return "";
-    }
-    boot_id = android::base::Trim(boot_id);
-
-    return "/dev/ashmem" + boot_id;
+    // The container ships a plain /dev/ashmem (memfd-backed shim) — there is no
+    // boot_id-suffixed node, so probing "/dev/ashmem<boot_id>" made __init_ashmem_rdev() fail
+    // everywhere and no genuine ashmem fd could ever pass __ashmem_is_ashmem(). Use the plain
+    // path, matching what __ashmem_open() already opens (bd remora-4ei.76). This also drops the
+    // /proc boot_id read, which a sandboxed process cannot perform anyway.
+    return "/dev/ashmem";
 }
 
 static bool __init_ashmem_rdev() {
@@ -197,6 +194,17 @@ static int __ashmem_check_failure(int fd, int result) {
 }
 
 static bool is_memfd_fd(int fd) {
+    // Ask the fd itself before consulting /proc. Chromium's renderer sandbox denies
+    // readlink(/proc/self/fd/*) with EPERM, and a memfd region is exactly what a sandboxed
+    // WebView renderer receives (sys.use_memfd=true) — so the /proc probe below misidentified
+    // every region, the ashmem fallback could not rescue it either (no ashmem node in the
+    // sandbox's /dev), and WebView composited black (bd remora-4ei.76). F_GET_SEALS answers
+    // >= 0 only for shmem-backed fds — every memfd, sealing-capable or not — while ashmem's
+    // chardev fd and ordinary file fds get EINVAL.
+    if (fcntl(fd, F_GET_SEALS) >= 0) {
+        return true;
+    }
+
     std::string fd_path = android::base::StringPrintf("/proc/self/fd/%d", fd);
     std::string result;
     if (!android::base::Readlink(fd_path, &result)) {
-- 
